This program implements a multiprocess word count utility in C that counts lines, 
words, and characters in a file. The file is divided into chunks based on the number 
of child processes specified by the user, and each chunk is processed concurrently by a 
separate child. Each child opens the file, seeks to its assigned offset, counts the data, 
and sends the results to the parent via pipes. The parent reads the results from the 
pipes and aggregates them to compute the total counts.

To simulate and handle failures, child processes may randomly crash based on a configurable crash rate. 
The parent monitors each child using waitpid(). If a child crashes or fails to return a complete result, 
the parent retries the job up to a maximum number of retries (MAX_RETRIES). If a job continues to fail 
after the maximum retries, the parent marks it complete and prints a warning that totals may be incomplete. 
This ensures fault tolerance while preventing the parent from crashing.

The program demonstrates multiprocessing, inter-process communication via pipes, 
and robust crash handling, while producing accurate totals whenever possible. 
Users can specify the number of processes and crash rate, making it suitable for performance 
testing and reliability demonstrations on Unix-like systems.
